  <artifactId>schema</artifactId>
  <name>schema</name>
  <description>Flyway migrations for the shared PostgreSQL database.</description>

  <!--
    Resources only: no Java, no dependencies.

    These migrations began in dispatch-api because it was the only service touching
    PostgreSQL. trip-service now owns the trip lifecycle and writes the same tables, and a
    schema that lives inside one of its two consumers is a schema one of them can change
    without the other noticing. Moving it here makes the database what it actually is —
    shared infrastructure that neither service owns alone — and gives both the identical
    migration set on the classpath.

    BOTH services run Flyway against it at startup, which is safe because they run the SAME
    migrations: Flyway takes a PostgreSQL advisory lock before migrating, so a concurrent
    starter waits rather than racing, and whichever arrives second finds the history table
    already current and does nothing. The failure mode worth avoiding was never concurrency;
    it was two services holding two different ideas of what the schema is.
  -->
</project>
